WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.842 --> 00:00:02.675
AASTHA BHARDWAJ: Welcome,
my name is Aastha.

00:00:02.675 --> 00:00:05.970
And I'm an engineer
on the Cloud SQL team.

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:10.630
In this video, I'll show you
how to create, run, and manage

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:13.850
SQL instances using
Google Cloud SQL.

00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:17.630
Cloud SQL helps manage
mundane administrative tasks

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:20.330
for MySQL and
Postgres databases.

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:23.850
So you can focus
on what matters.

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:26.090
Let's walk through how
to create a Postgres

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:29.660
database on Cloud SQL and
connect to it from the Google

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:31.160
Cloud Shell.

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:34.310
You can set up an instance
in just a few steps.

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:36.030
We'll start with the basics.

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:38.870
And I'll point out some options
for additional configuration

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:40.780
along the way.

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:45.390
We'll start by navigating to
Cloud SQL, in the GCP console,

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:48.420
and clicking Create Instance.

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:53.010
We'll select Postgres
and press Next.

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:55.980
We'll give the instance a name.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:58.860
And let's not forget to set
the password for our default

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:00.420
account.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:03.570
This instance will
be created in the US.

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:05.540
And we'll leave
the zone set to Any

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:08.930
so Cloud SQL can choose for us.

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:11.950
Let's take a look at the
configuration options.

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:17.650
You can specify Instant Size,
High Availability, and more.

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:21.280
For this demo, I'll
choose four CPU cores

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:24.280
and then maximize the memory.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:27.280
Maximizing memory is a
good choice for Postgres,

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:29.710
because it can take
advantage of the extra memory

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:31.330
for performance.

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:34.840
That said, don't worry too
much about initial sizing.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:38.530
You can scale the
instance up or down later.

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:42.460
Let's increase the storage
capacity to 200 gigabytes,

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:46.660
because increasing disk capacity
also increases performance.

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:49.710
Check out the performance
calculator below.

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:52.050
As I add capacity,
my disk performance

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:54.750
increases, which will
allow my database

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:57.450
to process more
operations per second.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:59.250
And I'll enable
Automatic Storage

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:01.470
Increase so that I
don't accidentally

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:04.200
run out of disk space.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.950
Lastly, let's ensure that the
database will automatically

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:10.440
provide isolation from failures.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:13.140
We can do that by enabling
the high availability

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:16.890
configuration, which, for
Postgres, will automatically

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:20.500
failover to another zone
in the case of an outage.

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:23.410
Now that we are happy with the
configuration of our instance,

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:24.990
let's kick off the creation.

00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:27.870
It may take a few minutes
for your instance to create.

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:31.080
This is a great time to
grab a quick cup of coffee.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:33.510
You can also check the
status of your instance

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:36.010
by navigating to
your instance list.

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:39.180
Once you see a green check
mark, your instance is ready.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:42.000
Looks like our instance
has finished creating.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.040
Let's open the Details
page to continue.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:47.970
There are many ways to
connect to this instance.

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:50.250
Within GCP, for
example, we could

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:54.270
use App Engine, Compute
Engine, or Container Engine,

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:57.450
but we could also connect,
from just about anywhere,

00:02:57.450 --> 00:03:01.800
by authorizing IP addresses
or using the Cloud SQL Proxy.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:05.970
For this demo, I'll connect
using the Google Cloud Shell.

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:08.400
Cloud Shell provides
command line access

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:12.360
to all of your cloud resources,
such as Cloud SQL instances,

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:15.250
directly from the browser.

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:20.720
To open the Cloud Shell, click
Connect Using Cloud Shell.

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:23.720
As you can see, the
relevant gcloud command

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:25.850
to connect to your
Cloud SQL instance

00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:27.780
has been pre-typed for us.

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:33.400
So all we need to
do is press Enter,

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:38.840
and then enter our root
password from before,

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:40.980
and now we're connected.

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:43.280
So let's add some data.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:46.160
I'll create a table, and
we'll insert a few rows.

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:52.300
Finally, I'll make sure the
data is now in our database.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:56.660
And it is.

00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:58.270
Great.

00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:01.150
So to recap, in the
last few minutes,

00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:03.760
we just created a
Cloud SQL instance,

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:06.700
with high performance
and high availability,

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:09.850
and connected to it
with Cloud Shell.

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:13.330
Now that you've seen how to
get started, give it a try.

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:17.140
You can learn more about Google
Cloud SQL and our free trial

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:18.430
program here.

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:20.550
Thank you for watching.

